[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Canvas and Custom Graphics Rendering in SwiftUI"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"SwiftUI provides the Canvas view for immediate-mode, high-performance 2D custom graphics rendering. Unlike traditional drawing via paths and shape views, the Canvas view gives you direct access to a GraphicsContext, allowing you to draw shapes, images, and text with precise control over rendering parameters."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Canvas View"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"At its core, a SwiftUI Canvas takes a closure with two parameters: context and size. The context is of type GraphicsContext, which acts as your drawing surface, while the size represents the available dimensions of the canvas view."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"Canvas { context, size in
    // Drawing operations go here
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Drawing Basic Shapes"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"To draw a basic shape such as a circle or rectangle, you first instantiate a Path object, define its geometry, and then instruct the context to stroke or fill it."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"Canvas { context, size in
    var path = Path()
    path.addEllipse(in: CGRect(x: 50, y: 50, width: 200, height: 200))

    context.fill(
        path,
        with: .color(.blue)
    )
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Advantages of Canvas"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Performance: Canvas is built on top of Metal, rendering complex scenes much faster than traditional view hierarchies."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Effects and Filters: You can easily apply shadows, blurs, and blend modes across multiple drawing layers."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Symbols: Canvas supports reusable symbols, allowing you to draw complex sub-views efficiently multiple times."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Applying Transformations"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"GraphicsContext supports affine transformations such as scaling, rotation, and translation. These modifications apply to all subsequent drawing commands until the context state is reset or modified."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"Canvas { context, size in
    context.translateBy(x: size.width / 2, y: size.height / 2)
    context.rotate(by: .degrees(45))

    let rect = CGRect(x: -50, y: -50, width: 100, height: 100)
    let path = Path(rect)

    context.fill(path, with: .color(.red))
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Canvas view bridges the gap between high-level declarative SwiftUI layouts and low-level custom graphics rendering. By leveraging GraphicsContext, you can build custom data visualizations, charts, and interactive graphics with exceptional performance."}]
